# aegis-base-nginx — the FIRST base image aegis OWNS: alpine + the
# distribution's nginx package, listening on 8080, running as UID 101.
# It is what the static fronts (portafolio, blog, ejemplo, and every
# `estatico` service an organization declares) put in their FROM.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE REPLACES, AND WHY
# ────────────────────────────────
# Until 2026-08-26 the static fronts stood on nginx-unprivileged, a
# THIRD-PARTY image mirrored into the internal registry. Mirroring it
# was already a repair: before 2026-08-09 the fronts pulled it from
# Docker Hub on every build, and build 38 of the portfolio died on a
# Hub timeout — while we were signing the RESULTING artifact with the
# aegis key without anyone having looked at the base underneath, which
# was pinned by tag. The mirror closed that hole: a base pulled once,
# by digest, scanned and signed like our own code.
#
# What the mirror could not do is PATCH the base. That became the whole
# story on 2026-08-26. OpenSSL published CVE-2026-14456 (a QUIC denial
# of service in libcrypto3; fixed in 3.5.8-r0, the mirrored image
# carried 3.5.7-r0). Alpine 3.22 shipped openssl 3.5.8-r0 on
# 2026-08-25. Upstream nginx had NOT rebuilt its image: both candidate
# tags, the one we mirrored and the next one, carried the vulnerable
# library. The scan is blocking, rightly so, so for days EVERY deploy
# of EVERY static front failed at the scan stage — over one library
# the fronts never even call (they serve plain HTTP behind the edge).
# The fix existed, in the distribution, one `apk upgrade` away, and we
# had no place to run it.
#
# This file is that place. An owned base is `alpine + apk upgrade +
# apk add nginx`: the distribution's package with the distribution's
# patch cadence, rebuilt by the base-images job the morning image-watch
# finds a fixable CVE, and propagated by digest to every consumer.
#
# WHAT A CONSUMER MAY ASSUME (the contract, asserted by the job before
# the image is pushed):
#   - it listens on 8080 (a tenant's NetworkPolicy admits only
#     edge → 8080; an image on port 80 starts fine and NEVER receives
#     traffic — the silent failure);
#   - it runs as UID/GID 101 (tenant namespaces are PSS `restricted`:
#     a root container is rejected at admission, and a non-numeric
#     USER fails runAsNonRoot because the kubelet cannot prove it);
#   - `COPY --chown=101:101 dist/ /usr/share/nginx/html/` works;
#   - a `server{}` copied to /etc/nginx/conf.d/default.conf is what
#     gets served, and it must keep `listen 8080`.
# That is nginx-unprivileged's contract, kept on purpose: the consumers
# changed one FROM line and nothing else.

# The FROM is pinned by TAG against the INTERNAL registry (the seed
# cannot know an instance's internal digest; the instance's
# mirror-images job pins the source by digest, and the build event
# records what this tag resolved to as `base_digest`). The minor here
# is the single source of the image's tag: the job derives
# `<minor>-<build>` from this line, the way ci-images does.
FROM registry.registry-system.svc.cluster.local:5000/alpine:3.22

# ORDER MATTERS, and every step below has a reason:
#
# 1. uid/gid 101 are created BEFORE `apk add nginx`. The package's
#    pre-install script creates the `nginx` user if it does not exist,
#    with the first free system uid — 100, not 101. Creating 101 first
#    turns the package's adduser into a no-op, and every chown the
#    package does lands on 101: the uid every consumer's
#    `COPY --chown=101:101` already assumes.
# 2. The `[ "$(id -u nginx)" = 101 ]` assertion. If the assumption
#    about the package is ever wrong (a future release renumbers, or
#    creates the user differently), the BUILD breaks here, with this
#    line in the log — instead of four tenant pods failing at once with
#    a permission error on a file the browser never sees.
# 3. `apk upgrade` BEFORE `apk add`. The mirrored alpine is frozen by
#    digest: its packages are whatever they were the day it was
#    mirrored. The upgrade is where today's patched packages come in —
#    it is the line that would have carried openssl 3.5.8-r0 on
#    2026-08-25, and it is the line image-watch rebuilds this image to
#    run.
# 4. Logs to stdout/stderr through symlinks, as the official images
#    do: the pod's log is what Vector reads; a file in the container
#    is read by nobody.
# 5. Everything nginx touches at runtime is chowned to 101; the temp
#    paths are NOT here — they live under /tmp (see nginx.conf) because
#    tenant pods run with a read-only root filesystem.
RUN addgroup -g 101 -S nginx \
 && adduser -u 101 -D -S -H -h /var/cache/nginx -s /sbin/nologin -G nginx -g nginx nginx \
 && apk upgrade --no-cache \
 && apk add --no-cache nginx \
 && [ "$(id -u nginx)" = 101 ] && [ "$(id -g nginx)" = 101 ] \
 && mkdir -p /usr/share/nginx/html /etc/nginx/conf.d /var/cache/nginx /var/lib/nginx /var/log/nginx \
 && ln -sf /dev/stdout /var/log/nginx/access.log && ln -sf /dev/stderr /var/log/nginx/error.log \
 && chown -R 101:101 /usr/share/nginx/html /etc/nginx/conf.d /var/cache/nginx /var/log/nginx /var/lib/nginx

# OUR OWN nginx.conf, not the package's. Alpine's stock nginx.conf
# includes `/etc/nginx/conf.d/*.conf` in the ROOT context (its
# http-context include is `/etc/nginx/http.d/`). Every consumer copies
# a `server{}` to /etc/nginx/conf.d/default.conf — nginx-unprivileged's
# layout — and with the stock file nginx would refuse to start:
# "server" directive is not allowed here. The base's nginx.conf
# includes conf.d INSIDE http{}, exactly where the consumers expect it.
COPY nginx.conf /etc/nginx/nginx.conf
COPY default.conf /etc/nginx/conf.d/default.conf
COPY index.html /usr/share/nginx/html/index.html

# The build-time contract for the include above: the sample
# default.conf is a `server{}` in conf.d, so if nginx.conf ever
# includes conf.d in the wrong context, `nginx -t` fails HERE, not in
# the consumer's pod.
RUN nginx -t

EXPOSE 8080
# SIGQUIT is nginx's graceful stop: in-flight requests finish, then the
# workers exit. SIGTERM (the default) is a fast shutdown.
STOPSIGNAL SIGQUIT
# Numeric on purpose (see the contract above). There is no `user`
# directive in nginx.conf: a non-root master cannot switch user, and
# with one there nginx logs a warning on every start.
USER 101
CMD ["nginx", "-g", "daemon off;"]
